/* Logic: 
Approach 1: Using push_back()

Logic:

1. Find the size of the input vector nums.
2. Create an answer vector and copy all elements of nums into it.
3. Traverse the original vector from the last index to the first.
4. Append each element to the answer vector using push\_back().
5. Return the answer vector.

Approach 2: Using Indexing

Logic:

1. Find the size of the input vector nums.
2. Create an answer vector of size 2\*n.
3. Traverse the original vector from index 0 to n-1.
4. Copy the original elements into the first half of the answer vector.
5. Store the elements in reverse order in the second half using ans\[i+n\] = nums\[n-i-1\].
6. Return the answer vector.

Conclusion:
Both approaches have the same time and space complexity.
Approach 1 uses push\_back() to append elements, 
whereas Approach 2 uses indexing to directly place elements at their required positions.
*/

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> concatWithReverse(vector<int>& nums) {
        // Get the size of the input vector
        int n = nums.size();

        // Copy the original vector into the answer vector
        vector<int> ans = nums;

        // Traverse the original vector from the last index to the first
        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {

            // Append each element to the answer vector in reverse order
            ans.push_back(nums[i]);
        }

        // Return the concatenated vector (original + reverse)
        return ans;
    }
};
